{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011Cf9wttS1ZdxeDMjcZfg7b","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All eight tests pass, and I kept the adjacency-set representation.\n\nChanges, all in `netgraph/graph.py`:\n- **add_node** appends an empty neighbour set and returns the new index, so growth is O(1).\n- **remove_node** subtracts the node's degree from the edge counter, deletes its set, then rewrites every other set: discard the removed id and shift ids above it down by one. A self-loop on the removed node is covered because the loop sits in its own set and counts once in its degree.\n- **edges** emits each edge once by keeping pairs where the first id is at most the second, which also captures self-loops, then sorts.\n\n```\npython -m pytest -q\n8 passed in 0.03s\n```\n\nI also checked cases the tests skip, such as self-loops on both the removed node and a higher node that gets relabelled. Counts and the edge list came out correct.\n\nOn the matrix side note: deleting a row and column is a clean picture, but the adjacency-set version costs the same O(nodes + edges) for relabelling and keeps memory linear, so switching representations wouldn't buy anything here."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":434,"cache_read_input_tokens":24596,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":434},"output_tokens":356,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"719ac66f-bc70-40d3-894f-f4ac12a65082","uuid":"81927576-b204-488a-b61d-1d37101ee3e4","timestamp":"2026-09-18T00:20:08.922Z","request_id":"req_011Cf9wtt3SEwAMSKtWo5ynD"}
